        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Ancient Near East</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Raphia</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">22 June 217 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the small town of Rafah (Raphia) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, on the coast road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Gaza, in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>; modern Rafah</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV Philopator</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, Ptolemy had 70,000 infantry, 5,000 cavalry and 73 elephants, and Antiochus 62,000 infantry, 6,000 cavalry and 102 elephants</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Ptolemaic army drove the Seleucid army from the field, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> asked for a truce to bury his dead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a>'s victory secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
