    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>It is the only known battle in which African and Asian elephants were used against each other, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> says the larger Indian elephants easily routed the Africans.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> routed the Ptolemaic horse facing him and rode off in pursuit, but the Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a> drove back the Seleucid phalanxes in his absence and his army broke before he returned.</li><li>The large native Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> that helped win the battle is said to have given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> a new self-confidence, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> counted among the causes of the secession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> from 207.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>'s army included 102 Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a>'s 73 African ones, the only known battle in which the two kinds met.</p></aside>
    </section>

