    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">219 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-syrian-war">Fourth Syrian War</a> begins between Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Both armies are on campaign through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and meet near the town of Rafah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Theodotus the Aetolian enters the Ptolemaic camp to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a> but finds the king's tent empty</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After five days of skirmishing the two armies are drawn up and the battle is fought</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">217 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Priests meet at Memphis to give thanks for the victory, and a stele honours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> in Greek as well as Egyptian</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">207 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> secedes under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> Hugronaphor and Ankhmakis, a separate kingdom lasting nearly twenty years</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> defeats the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a> V Epiphanes at Panium and recaptures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a> and Judea</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-raphia" data-v="4e48858234" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies drawn up</b> After five days of skirmishing both kings arrayed their troops. Each put the phalanx in the centre, the light troops and mercenaries beside it, the elephants in front of them and the cavalry on the wings.</li><li><b>The elephants charge and the Ptolemaic ones panic</b> The elephants on both wings moved to charge. The smaller Ptolemaic African elephants retreated in panic before the larger Indian ones and ran through the infantry behind them, which fell into disorder.</li><li><b>Antiochus charges the Ptolemaic horse</b> Antiochus led his cavalry to the right, rode past the left wing of the Ptolemaic elephants and charged the enemy horse.</li><li><b>The phalanxes engage, and the Ptolemaic right wins</b> The two phalanxes closed in a stiff and chaotic fight. Ptolemy rode to the centre to urge on his phalanx, which had Egyptians beside the Macedonians. On the far right the Ptolemaic cavalry routed its opponents.</li><li><b>Antiochus routs the Ptolemaic horse and pursues</b> Antiochus routed the Ptolemaic horse facing him and pursued the fleeing enemy in a mass, believing that he had won the day.</li><li><b>The Ptolemaic phalanx drives the Seleucids back</b> The Ptolemaic phalanx drove the Seleucid phalanx back. Antiochus tried to ride back, but by then his troops were routed and could not be regrouped, and the battle was over.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator.jpg" alt="Ptolemy IV Philopator" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV Philopator</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Octadrachm_Ptolemy_IV_BM_CMBMC33.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> were the two largest Hellenistic kingdoms of the 3rd century BCE, and they repeatedly fought for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in a series of conflicts known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-wars">Syrian Wars</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-syrian-war">Fourth Syrian War</a> began in 219 BCE. Egypt was then ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a>, who was king and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, and the Seleucid Empire by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a>.</p><p>The prize was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>. Theodotus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a>, who had served Ptolemy, betrayed him and handed much of Coele-Syria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> over to Antiochus, and he afterwards commanded the Silver Shields in Antiochus's army. By 217 BCE both armies were on campaign through Syria, and they met near the small Syrian town of Rafah.</p><p>Antiochus first set up his camp at a distance of 10 stadia, about 2 kilometres, from his adversary's, and then moved to only 5 stadia, about 1 kilometre. The fighting began with a series of small skirmishes around the edge of each army. One night Theodotus, the former officer of Ptolemy, slipped into the Ptolemaic camp and made for what he took to be the king's tent in order to assassinate him. Ptolemy was not there, and the plot failed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Seleucid army, according to the figures given for its contingents, was made up of 5,000 lightly armed Dahae, Carmanians and Cilicians under Byttacus the Macedonian, 10,000 phalangites, the Argyraspides or Silver Shields, under Theodotus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a>, and 20,000 Macedonian phalangites under Nicarchus and Theodotus Hemiolius. There were also 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, Cissians, Cadusii and Carmanians, 10,000 Arabians under Zabdibelus, 5,000 Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under Hippolochus the Thessalian, and Cretans and Neocretans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> had 4,000 horsemen under his nephew Antipater and 2,000 under Themison, and 102 Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> under Philip and Myischos.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a> had just ended a major recruitment and retraining plan with the help of many mercenary generals. His force included 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hypaspists">hypaspists</a>, the Agema, 2,000 peltasts, 25,000 Macedonian phalangites, 8,000 Greek mercenaries, Cretan and Neocretan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>, and 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> under his chief minister Sosibius, trained in the Macedonian way to fight alongside the Macedonians. His Household Cavalry numbered 700, and the local and Libyan horse another 2,300. He had 73 elephants of African stock.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, Ptolemy had 70,000 infantry, 5,000 cavalry and 73 war elephants, and Antiochus 62,000 infantry, 6,000 cavalry and 102 elephants.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After five days of skirmishing, the two kings decided to array their troops for battle. Both placed their phalangites in the centre. Next to them they put the lightly armed troops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, in front of which stood the elephants, and further out on the wings the cavalry. The kings spoke to their soldiers and took their places, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a> on his left and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> on his right wing.</p><p>At the start the elephants on the wings of both armies charged. Ptolemy's smaller African elephants retreated in panic before the larger Indian ones and ran back through the friendly infantry behind them, causing disorder. Antiochus led his cavalry to the right and rode past the left wing of the Ptolemaic elephants to charge the enemy horse. The two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanxes</a> then engaged in a stiff and chaotic fight. Antiochus had the Silver Shields, while Ptolemy's Macedonians were strengthened by the Egyptian phalanx. The right wing of Ptolemy was retreating and wheeling to protect itself from the panicked elephants, and Ptolemy rode to the centre to urge his phalanx on. On the far right of the Ptolemaic line, his cavalry was routing its opponents.</p><p>Antiochus routed the Ptolemaic horse facing him and pursued the fleeing enemy in a mass, believing he had won the day. The Ptolemaic phalanxes in the meantime drove the Seleucid phalanxes back. When Antiochus rode back his troops were routed and could no longer be regrouped.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the battle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a> wanted to regroup and make camp outside Raphia, but most of his men had already found refuge inside the town, and he was forced to enter it himself. He then marched to Gaza and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a> for the customary truce to bury the dead, which was granted.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> lost a little under 10,000 infantry, about 300 horse and 5 elephants, and 4,000 men were taken prisoner. The Ptolemaic losses were 1,500 infantry, 700 horse and 16 elephants. Most of the Ptolemies' elephants were captured by the Seleucids.</p><p>Ptolemy's victory secured the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, but it was only a respite. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-panium">Battle of Panium</a> in 200 BCE Antiochus defeated the army of Ptolemy's young son, Ptolemy V Epiphanes, and recaptured Coele-Syria and Judea.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The figures for the two armies and for the losses are those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, and the descriptions of the elephants and of the king's speeches to his troops also come from him. The elephants have been a subject of study. Polybius describes the Asian elephants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus</a>, brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, as larger and stronger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a>'s African ones, and it was once theorised that these were African forest elephants. Recent DNA research has suggested instead that they were African bush elephants, culled from a population of smaller animals still found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, and another possibility is the now extinct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-african-elephant">North African elephant</a>, about 2.4 metres high at the shoulder.</p><p>The stele that records the convocation of priests at Memphis in November 217, held to give thanks for the victory, survives, inscribed in Greek and in hieroglyphic and demotic Egyptian. Some biblical commentators see the battle as the one referred to in Daniel 11:11.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was one of the largest of the Hellenistic kingdoms and of the ancient world, and it determined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>, although only until 200 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy</a> owed his victory in part to a properly equipped and trained native Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, which for the first time formed a large proportion of his phalangites and ended his manpower problems.</p><p>The self-confidence the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> gained was credited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> as one of the causes of the secession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> in 207 to 186 BCE under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> Hugronaphor and Ankhmakis, who created a separate kingdom that lasted nearly twenty years. The battle marks a turning point in Ptolemaic history. The native Egyptian element in the administration and culture of the 2nd century BCE grew in influence, driven in part by the major role Egyptians had played in the battle and in part by the financial pressures on the state, made worse by the cost of the war itself.</p><p>The Memphis stele gives Ptolemy full pharaonic honours in the Greek texts as well as the Egyptian ones for the first time, and this later became the norm.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Raphia on 22 June 217 BCE the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> defeated that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, in a battle where African and Asian elephants met. Egypt kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>, and the large native Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> changed its politics.</em></p>

